    "result_text": "The full suite is green. BUILD SUCCESS with all tests passing (2 run, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `locality` derivation from an owner's city, using the fixed table Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD, defaulting to `UNKNOWN`.\n\nChanges:\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `locality` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field in the `GET`/`POST` responses.\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/Locality.java`** (new) \u2014 a standalone helper holding the city\u2192region table and `Locality.of(city)`.\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added `@Mapping(target = \"locality\", expression = \"java(...Locality.of(owner.getCity()))\")` alongside the other derived fields.\n\n### One gotcha worth flagging\nMy first attempt put the derivation as a `static String localityOf(String)` method *on the `OwnerMapper` interface*. MapStruct scans the mapper interface for candidate mapping methods and picked it up as an implicit `String \u2192 String` converter, then applied it to **every** string property \u2014 including `telephone`, which became `\"UNKNOWN\"` and failed the entity's E.164 validation with a 500 at persist time. Moving the helper to a separate class (which MapStruct does not scan) fixed it. That's why `locality` derivation belongs in its own class rather than on the mapper.",
